#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "e4f2ef63-cc21-5aeb-b1c4-6a7a4d40e81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12411" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/1eecba17a7af16086022541eaab52f0755f959cb3f0e5059d61322bbbd81c9c4.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$ for $y \\geq 0$ and $z \\leq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/9415dd50c82e3f5f4e47e26f459dda1315f819348e87ff97a4b05b1c27b59891.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}, \\: \n{0} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\pi}, \\: \n \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}2} \\leq \\phi \\leq {\\pi}   \n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "68b95ae7-c0ac-590c-a764-0500455ca70c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12412" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/0437b193a24fadb4e43ec449e685abd9b8a220ff72019d6c5b1d242e6eb49942.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/449c0a320c59fc072c7ca38307d9929910b1dfa432d7e3afdf11824e97270dc5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: \n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq  \\pi\n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175738 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "78981c7f-ec42-5eac-afb8-145bd2a7e66c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8811" :question/problem "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/a60ddaebcf32cd8d69624b79f34d96d8ca1c18ec59c8bc443a95e235dedfae9e.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175739 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "bc7cbddf-7c15-5762-b035-cbd68a1f25f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8812" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho=5$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5.$ On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/4a8c82e617e1d040c61f0f7fca8dd35985005df553aaa53b95e4b14871df6d8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988c0c53-e5a4-5f3e-add0-18ee4a4f054b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173311} {:answer/id #uuid "a71bed2c-0ee9-5924-9fee-80c92e9f69c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173312} {:answer/id #uuid "c03ba347-80cc-570d-bdfd-17a999b994fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173313} {:answer/id #uuid "0663dd61-8117-58e8-8294-916c80615a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173314} {:answer/id #uuid "3c421c89-baa7-58b0-9858-c0ae6e816547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71bed2c-0ee9-5924-9fee-80c92e9f69c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173312} :answer-field/id #uuid "21447bc9-f05d-5935-be7c-f70c0e1747cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f0566ac-a9c9-553f-9d54-910954e3b64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102949" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/e542606b09d564f450aa9c2f4e2de5bf8dc197644a094c37420ee1b881f14e1f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $2.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7963f00b-1548-56cd-9b47-e08247a52cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312316} {:answer/id #uuid "cbea4182-f6a8-5d15-8353-409959ed3837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312317} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc764d0-8e18-5274-bc0d-7d4c8fd54cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312318} {:answer/id #uuid "443f1e23-6dc9-584f-81e9-6b18f7b59656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312319} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9cf9a3-5496-5e59-8a2a-e6a851557bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9cf9a3-5496-5e59-8a2a-e6a851557bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312320} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd2dff53-5112-5a0a-ba5b-bcfea601833c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97712f28-e4fa-5d27-9d28-9d9d405f7678" :question/math-academy-id "q-135246" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/1fa98148cf4302a5524ade655ee09a3f74d248ba766849f62a71f3ec85e860c6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caffe55f-6d82-598e-8368-334e411b06b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312323} {:answer/id #uuid "f714ea75-44d5-5b64-b52e-11cb36157f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312324} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e892a3-8b1c-5c61-8009-9e348e625bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =9\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312325} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c9dc4b-6a7a-527a-91f4-240912205d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =3\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312326} {:answer/id #uuid "891b9261-3730-50f1-8302-a3baee6f9658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caffe55f-6d82-598e-8368-334e411b06b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312323} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ea3213-da61-55fe-aa5e-4c8852646075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d71849e3-838f-5ed8-adc0-fc82a6f2a5f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-135247" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4023e15-6dd6-5dda-8043-81033682a28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312330} {:answer/id #uuid "992c9f69-8ac7-5063-b3f1-dc6f25eee956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312331} {:answer/id #uuid "77a2b37e-71b4-554d-bb77-e9446917f1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312332} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc09b8e-036b-50df-92c6-75b8ececdb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312333} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdfaf87-1cfe-56ad-a56c-aac5589e3d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a2b37e-71b4-554d-bb77-e9446917f1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312332} :answer-field/id #uuid "457ca424-084f-5ba1-aa3a-c1a99fda0e62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d239b0f-0cd5-555b-8f1c-70f958757512" :question/math-academy-id "q-135254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/61b3e9163769acbcb5804ccd2a5cbfed9cbbf2f0fc113ff39b64ea7153fbe2f4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $\\sqrt{2}.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff72d1b-b153-5849-9fa0-9f9e4f7e3513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312337} {:answer/id #uuid "a25c809e-c457-5049-ba8c-b210b2c042a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =\\frac{\\theta }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312338} {:answer/id #uuid "a99f4f2d-73a1-5f99-b324-42b153dd2126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312339} {:answer/id #uuid "459ffdfb-6368-5701-b7d1-11a1a75bb4e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312340} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6bae9d-a86b-5249-ad8e-657ac2768522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad6bae9d-a86b-5249-ad8e-657ac2768522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312341} :answer-field/id #uuid "12972fdb-d6a6-515e-870f-45235973639b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9d70e19-4ec6-510a-946b-fdc58cad22e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-135283" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/68dc8c639235c6dbc2fe9a238a3855327a0c7da337e5792b7896c30e19b2714e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f67c4a-bac8-5bb9-adc0-330b40f9f09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312344} {:answer/id #uuid "a001cbed-7455-51e7-8498-d0cfa43aae32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312345} {:answer/id #uuid "f186e26c-15c9-5e22-b8ff-079b4ebdaafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312346} {:answer/id #uuid "6746f371-31fa-52f1-b281-80e687150c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312347} {:answer/id #uuid "f56c0fa9-a5c3-5d2f-ae7d-58c8b294a7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6746f371-31fa-52f1-b281-80e687150c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312347} :answer-field/id #uuid "591464f2-fc7a-5036-a387-2957ce6bd1ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4204862-8d04-599c-b1c4-5e077bf6c058" :question/math-academy-id "q-135295" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/ee967c13fa7edaaba4d3a218abf20aa067cbd2251f2a008158c9c991c27344ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63a2477-e6e2-515f-ac14-e4a37606974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312351} {:answer/id #uuid "88b066f2-9175-58c8-ba69-70ef385ece97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312352} {:answer/id #uuid "52017f35-0d9a-5f9f-8051-775abaeded55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312353} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b6