onding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =8\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $8.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/35a2af1537995e83771267fc3ecf28816e9612fc11cead84bb9dbb06ac5e90c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d48090ef-223c-5060-8e26-b55a1d772ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312365} {:answer/id #uuid "da52c0aa-e322-53ed-9ff8-1b50d64089b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312366} {:answer/id #uuid "2f16f454-f67c-5c6f-a643-192706c88440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312367} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c5ee14-31b6-5e32-bd32-c103ca23e536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312368} {:answer/id #uuid "0b12f108-5a9c-51e5-90f3-31ed335582c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da52c0aa-e322-53ed-9ff8-1b50d64089b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5342f24-aeb7-586e-bce3-442a1af3ab4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88fdcb72-76af-50c0-96fa-8648d47767d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-135300" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5e7de8-9f53-508a-be7c-675ebcb4a969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312372} {:answer/id #uuid "4de19c08-f0ef-593b-9f24-2de318701f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312373} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9f6ec8-a412-5eb1-a50a-c83d72a7b953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312374} {:answer/id 