field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16441bd9-94c0-5ceb-96d3-9d7a55c035e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161826" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/a6cb0ee7062154a2489c7fa565fe6b96cd01e5f6e7a478cce39fafda0778c7a7.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/d7011de88ab3a4acb5125e5dd67e119f7cf8e1bf823c089b5f37632a5c54b971.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\pi /2,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\pi /2\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec5f701a-b33f-5d81-9d89-785021b3dfb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\l