0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/cca15d8d91ebfe643777122cf41a49f2bfe3bac901b30e160cad65eb6b4b102f.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db505b9b-a656-5557-8773-eae17a89a346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312414} {:answer/id #uuid "1b08d785-3b59-518e-9947-c7404ff596f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312415} {:answer/id #uuid "9c51b9aa-d45e-5513-8677-66e46c51d9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312416} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f3ccdb-d337-5232-bbd0-5072139e5a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312417} {:answer/id #uuid "5af55b41-dafa-5b1f-970d-c1f0b5a064a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b08d785-3b59-518e-9947-c7404ff596f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312415} :answer-field/id #uuid "0363f85c-09b5-599e-bb4e-c5a83e9ef289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8aaa93cc-15b4-56b8-8ae9-9d4c15dc969c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161844" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/3e28d562bb7edd9a067bb3ee2c7cae07dc3b76315f39eeed084bdb06682042a2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/70450f3fc9e9971a76e4458c5e020c1f264e739913fbb24d3e6ff22359542316.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d15d3a4-00d0-56e9-b659-9dc4de5f6b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312421} {:answer/id #uuid "2346c329-f2d8-5e96-9275-629533f90a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312422} {:answer/id #uuid "6d77e5f7-be39-5f1a-abbf-feb1a215615b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312423} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdfde05-9f5e-51f8-abe9-79f23af42287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312424} {:answer/id #uuid "0c583e81-9a38-55a5-977d-ff76021e6763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d77e5f7-be39-5f1a-abbf-feb1a215615b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312423} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c91a51d-bb41-5a53-a44a-271ae7ad9f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7566667-f808-5eb0-a535-838d177964ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-161850" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/dcd901b032075a4c4a22c991d9bb3e5e5ff49e7a2c91e7e840ede1516085f683.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/736c46d0121c646def56c6b8fede81a4155fffb97032facd75a6cdf29f98f65a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "727cef38-01da-50c0-b8d1-221f038465b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 16\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312428} {:answer/id #uuid "83ac28f8-af55-5fc1-b52d-f6810724d95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312429} {:answer/id #uuid "4644af07-49bc-58c5-964f-5d0a9d66fa3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312430} {:answer/id #uuid "af41d341-bb9b-5abe-9143-299d5759a444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312431} {:answer/id #uuid "58012ece-490c-570b-8dab-086e2a1b31d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ac28f8-af55-5fc1-b52d-f6810724d95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312429} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a20c4fd-e569-5c23-b333-df72545d5560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89bb74cb-9bc0-558f-a6e8-3a2a5f9d3a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161854" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/693290f788fac9ba63ef072c1d76a9c6bb8fce9cafbaeada5abb425262347186.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8z,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/f508e47498e6fa625fef03bf42051410362763ee0b0731c776d207ca8647559f.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474f594d-a646-5a15-8ff6-29147fdd4e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312435} {:answer/id #uuid "14693549-eed6-5820-9834-0840318668ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312436} {:answer/id #uuid "17809735-b771-52cc-acfe-a7333a5e42b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{25}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312437} {:answer/id #uuid "abe3ec5b-38a0-578c-a645-a53e6ddca472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312438} {:answer/id #uuid "2c39d174-69b3-5b9d-a7ca-59a090fae88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 25,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe3ec5b-38a0-578c-a645-a53e6ddca472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312438} :answer-field/id #uuid "53734b62-4d18-5208-9dbf-c83bd365bf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "084fb25a-6bf4-5dd3-8597-2be6a35f1998" :question/math-academy-id "q-161882" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/cc5c179ec93879776b7f033f8a62674fcf272ca0b1270cef0902905192d613f4.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $\\frac{5}{2}$ centered at $(0,0,-\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/22924aa3079443eb0c712afbb80bbe1b58407cec297a8ef4c90fb0e69aec9735.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25860b39-6a28-51a7-a553-a8f20740d94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312442} {:answer/id #uuid "91f22c3c-e6d2-5ba8-b74e-623403b216d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312443} {:answer/id #uuid "839c52cd-d562-58c1-acb5-050d78d7946b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312444} {:answer/id #uuid "aa09d4d6-df1f-51fc-92e8-4d446a2b60e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312445} {:answer/id #uuid "78c44e76-0f5e-5d8e-bb56-463b975e9fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f22c3c-e6d2-5ba8-b74e-623403b216d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312443} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc830359-a1dc-5a66-9e81-6b56a9320ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a0c002a-2b5a-5cd3-a76c-858d2ccb4533" :question/math-academy-id "q-161888" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/f65b6429452cd985e064734d6e7e62c63abb58e2e481e5b4781635be8d4f1d8e.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/7d161ad5adc8f99f81634049c487f299f84edde255b2f08aa3d1dd4060f99e75.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$"}]}
